🔄 How services communicate without Eureka
When you're not using Eureka or any service discovery, you have to rely on manual configuration like:

✅ 1. Hardcoded URLs/IPs
Each service must know the address of other services in advance.

📌 Example:

// UserService is calling OrderService
String url = "http://localhost:8082/orders";
ResponseEntity<List<Order>> orders = restTemplate.getForEntity(url, List.class);
This works if the address is fixed.

But it breaks if:

The address or port changes

There are multiple instances (load balancing)

The services are running in different environments (dev/stage/prod)

✅ 2. Using Environment Properties
You can keep the URLs in application.properties or .yml files:

order-service.url=http://localhost:8082
But again:

You must manually update URLs for every environment.

No auto-registration or scaling support.

✅ 3. API Gateway Approach
You use an API Gateway (like Spring Cloud Gateway, Zuul, or NGINX) to route requests:

routes:
  - id: order-service
    uri: http://localhost:8082
    predicates:
      - Path=/orders/**
Still:

You have to configure every service path manually

No discovery or failover

✅ 4. Containerization Tools (like Docker Compose or Kubernetes)
If you use Docker:

services:
  userservice:
    build: .
    ports:
      - "8081:8081"
  orderservice:
    build: .
    ports:
      - "8082:8082"
Services can access each other by container name:


http://orderservice:8082/orders
But it's still limited unless you use Kubernetes service discovery or Eureka/Consul.

❌ Problems Without Eureka:
❌ No automatic scaling/discovery

❌ Difficult to maintain hardcoded IPs/URLs

❌ No health checks or service status tracking

❌ No built-in load balancing

✅ What Eureka Adds
| Feature           | Without Eureka | With Eureka                          |
| ----------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Hardcoded URLs    | ✅ Required     | ❌ Not needed                         |
| Auto-discovery    | ❌              | ✅                                    |
| Load balancing    | ❌ (manual)     | ✅ (via RestTemplate + @LoadBalanced) |
| Failover handling | ❌              | ✅                                    |
| Scaling support   | ❌              | ✅                                    |


🔁 Final Answer:
Without Eureka, services must communicate using hardcoded URLs or config files, which is error-prone and hard to scale.
Eureka removes this problem by providing dynamic service discovery and registration.





🧠 "Here, order-service is resolved by Eureka, not DNS" — What does it mean?
🔍 Normally:
In a typical setup without Eureka, when your code says:

String url = "http://order-service/api/orders";
Java (or Spring Boot) tries to find order-service using DNS resolution — like it would for a website (google.com → IP address).
 //// if still not understand look bellow

🟢 But with Eureka:
When you have this setup:

You register order-service as a service name with Eureka

You use RestTemplate or WebClient with @LoadBalanced

Spring doesn’t call DNS — instead, it:

Looks up the service name (order-service) in Eureka Registry

Gets a list of available instances of that service

Picks one (using load balancing)

Sends the request to that instance

✅ So "http://order-service" is resolved by Eureka, not the operating system’s DNS.

✅ What you need to make it work:
In application.yml:

spring.application.name: user-service

eureka.client.service-url.defaultZone: http://localhost:8761/eureka

With @LoadBalanced bean:

@Bean
@LoadBalanced // This makes Eureka service names resolvable
public RestTemplate restTemplate() {
    return new RestTemplate();
}
Then you can call other services by name:

ResponseEntity<String> response = restTemplate.getForEntity("http://order-service/api/orders", String.class);
🧾 Summary:
| Without Eureka       | With Eureka                                   |
| -------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| URL resolves via DNS | URL resolves via Eureka Registry              |
| Must hardcode IPs    | Can use service names (e.g., `order-service`) |
| No load balancing    | Load balancing via Spring Cloud               |



🌐 What is DNS Resolution?
DNS = Domain Name System

DNS resolution is the process of converting a name (like google.com) into an IP address (like 142.250.190.78), which the computer uses to connect.

💡 Real-World Example (without Eureka)
You open your browser and type:

https://google.com
👉 Your system asks DNS: “What is the IP address of google.com?”
👉 DNS replies: “It is 142.250.190.78”
👉 Now your browser connects to that IP.

📌 So:

google.com → DNS → 142.250.190.78 (resolved name)

🧱 Now in Java (Microservices)
You write in your code:

String url = "http://order-service/api/orders";
👉 Normally, Java (or Spring) tries to resolve order-service:

It thinks order-service is a hostname, like google.com.

It looks in:

Your local DNS settings

Your hosts file

Or asks the network DNS server

🟥 If it doesn't find order-service there → it fails with UnknownHostException.

✅ What Eureka Changes
If you're using Eureka + @LoadBalanced:

@LoadBalanced
@Bean
public RestTemplate restTemplate() {
    return new RestTemplate();
}
Then Spring does NOT ask DNS. Instead:

It asks Eureka: “Who is order-service?”

Eureka replies: “I have 2 instances of order-service:

http://192.168.1.10:8082

http://192.168.1.11:8082”

Spring picks one and calls it

🎉 So order-service is resolved by Eureka, not by DNS.

🔁 Summary
| Without Eureka                           | With Eureka                                 |
| ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Java asks DNS: "What's `order-service`?" | Java asks Eureka: "What's `order-service`?" |
| Fails unless hostname is known           | Works dynamically                           |
| You must manually manage IPs             | Eureka handles registration and lookup      |
